    chars: 2757,
    text: "Cutting false reps, part 3 of 3.\n\nWorkout Director counts reps from the phone camera. Most of the work in counting reps from a pose stream is not counting the things that are not reps.\n\nNothing counts until the move's joints have been seen on every frame for half a second, with no reading jumping more than 40 degrees in a fifteenth of a second. Flicker mid set disarms the counter until the next steady half second.\n\nThe App Store notes for version 1.0.86 list what changed: half reps, walking, standing up and getting into position no longer add reps.\n\nState machine\n\nThe counter has three states: waiting, rest and work. It waits until the angle first reaches the rest line, so a clip that opens at the bottom does not count. Push ups need two readings past the work line before the bottom counts.\n\nGates on the return\n\nA return to the top is refused if the rep travelled less than 80% of the gap between the lines (at least 20 degrees), took under 0.5 seconds (0.6 for deadlifts), or came too soon after the last rep: 0.9 seconds for squats, 1.0 for deadlifts, 0.8 for push ups.\n\nThe return must also clear the work line by 60% of that gap, at least 20 degrees. For a deadlift, that is back above 146 degrees.\n\nShape and posture\n\nA squat only counts if the hips go down, the legs move together and the feet stay put. A row only counts once the lifter has been bent over and still for a moment, a pull up once they are hanging.\n\nSecond rep\n\nOn squats, curls, presses, rows and deadlifts, a first rep is held for up to 8 seconds and counts only when another follows. Then both count. A held rep that times out is dropped. On since 1.0.87.\n\nA benchmark in the code picked those five moves. On curls, false reps fell from 35.4 an hour to 3.4, at a cost of 2.0% of real reps. Lunges would have lost 9.5%, so they count rep 1 at once.\n\nRange\n\nThe first counted rep records the lifter's range. Later work lines use a share of it, never easier than a fixed floor: 140 degrees for a deadlift, 125 for a squat, 130 for a push up.\n\nManual path and flags\n\nIf the camera can't see the lifter, minus and plus stay one tap away. Counting changes go out behind feature flags that can be turned off from the server without a new build.\n\nReferences\n\n1. Apple Vision, Detecting human body poses in images: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/vision/detecting-human-body-poses-in-images\n2. MediaPipe Pose Landmarker: https://ai.google.dev/edge/mediapipe/solutions/vision/pose_landmarker\n3. Workout Director on the App Store: https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/workout-director-gym-home/id6813645709\n4. How Workout Director is built, part 1: https://ashishkharche.com/notes/workout-director-1/\n\nMore notes on how Workout Director is built: https://ashishkharche.com/notes/",
